{
  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "lem:gap-near-optimality",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "For every \\(\\mathsf G_{\\nu}\\in\\mathcal G_p\\) and every strictly positive \\(\\varepsilon_{\\nu}>0\\), any certified rule \\(\\widetilde\\delta_{\\nu}\\) whose primal upper value and dual lower value differ by at most \\(\\varepsilon_{\\nu}\\) satisfies \\[0\\le R_{\\nu}(\\widetilde\\delta_{\\nu})-v_{\\nu}\\le\\varepsilon_{\\nu}.\\] In particular, \\(0\\le R(\\delta_m)-v\\le G_m\\) for every exchange iteration \\(m\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(\\mathsf G_{\\nu}\\)",
      "\\(p\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal G_p\\)",
      "\\(\\widetilde\\delta_{\\nu}\\)",
      "\\(\\varepsilon_{\\nu}\\)",
      "\\(R_{\\nu}\\)",
      "\\(v_{\\nu}\\)",
      "\\(R\\)",
      "\\(\\delta_m\\)",
      "\\(v\\)",
      "\\(G_m\\)",
      "\\(m\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:exchange-certificate",
      "def:stable-selector",
      "thm:complete-saddle-duality",
      "prop:certificate-convergence"
    ],
    "justification": "This isolates the algebraic certificate implication from the substantive continuity and experiment-transfer arguments.",
    "gap": "The inequality is weak-duality bookkeeping rather than a new stability theorem; stating it separately prevents the transfer claim from receiving novelty credit for an immediate step.",
    "consumer": "Every finite stopping tolerance becomes an auditable excess-regret bound before any perturbation or LAN approximation is invoked.",
    "proof_tex": "For the game \\(\\mathsf G_{\\nu}\\), let \\(L\\) and \\(U\\) be the certified dual lower and primal upper values. Weak duality from \\(\\mathrm{thm:complete-saddle-duality}\\) gives\n\\[\n L\\leq v_{\\nu}\\leq U=R_{\\nu}(\\widetilde\\delta_{\\nu}).\n\\]\nThe certificate and strict positivity required by \\(\\mathrm{def:stable-selector}\\) give \\(U-L\\leq\\varepsilon_{\\nu}\\), whence\n\\[\n 0\\leq R_{\\nu}(\\widetilde\\delta_{\\nu})-v_{\\nu}\n \\leq U-L\\leq\\varepsilon_{\\nu}.\n\\]\nAt exchange iteration \\(m\\), \\(U_m=R(\\delta_m)\\), \\(L_m\\leq v\\), and \\(G_m=U_m-L_m\\), so the identical subtraction gives \\(0\\leq R(\\delta_m)-v\\leq G_m\\). This is purely the primal--dual gap implication; no continuity or limiting assertion is used.",
